<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#assegai">Glossary</a> · Term · weapon</p><h1 class="enc-title">Assegai</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Assagai, Iklwa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An assegai is a polearm used for throwing, usually a light spear or javelin with a wooden handle and an iron tip. Various types were used all over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and it was the most common weapon there before the introduction of firearms; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a>, Xhosa and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni</a> peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> were noted for their use of it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingiswayo">Dingiswayo</a>, the Zulu ruler whose chief lieutenant was Shaka, popularised in war a shorter stabbing spear, traditionally a hunting spear, with a 610 mm shaft and a broad blade 300 mm long, known as the iklwa. The throwing spear was kept and hurled at enemy formations before close combat with the iklwa and a cowhide shield.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/assegai.jpg" alt="Assegai" width="330" height="614" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sagaies.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
